# The SPA's catch-all is gone.
#
# It read `/* /index.html 200`, which is what a client-side router needs: every
# path serves the homepage and Navigo decides what to show. Under Eleventy every
# page is a real file, and keeping the catch-all would turn every 404 — including
# a typo'd link to /privacy — into a silent homepage, which is worse than a 404.
#
# The old SPA route fragments, for anyone following a link that is now years old.
# /privacy is deliberately not a destination anyone is redirected to: it keeps its
# own URL and is simply not linked from the page (Nandan, 2026-08-25).

/overview   /         301
/studies    /         301
/software   /#code    301
/jobs       /         301

# docs.vlab.digital, folded into this site 2026-08-29 (D-008). The docs were a
# separate Hugo site on GitHub Pages; they are now /docs/ here, and every URL that
# subdomain ever served maps one-to-one onto a URL under it — /fly/reference/bails/
# became /docs/fly/reference/bails/ and so on for all 47.
#
# The source is an ABSOLUTE URL, which scopes the rule to that host. A bare `/*`
# here would swallow every path on vlab.digital itself.
#
# REQUIRES ONE DNS CHANGE, and this rule does nothing until it is made: add
# docs.vlab.digital as a domain alias on the Netlify site and point its DNS here.
# Until then the old GitHub Pages site keeps answering that host and the two
# diverge silently, which is the one failure mode worth watching for.
https://docs.vlab.digital/*   https://vlab.digital/docs/:splat   301!
http://docs.vlab.digital/*    https://vlab.digital/docs/:splat   301!
